00:01:01.946 --> 00:01:04.260
"What Every Body is
Saying" by Joe Navarro.

00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:05.776
Now, I love the catchy title in this book.

00:01:05.776 --> 00:01:08.390
"What Every Body is Saying."

00:01:08.390 --> 00:01:10.650
- Oh, okay, I see what you did there.

00:01:10.650 --> 00:01:13.500
- This is a book covering
human body language

00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:15.150
and I think this is very important

00:01:15.150 --> 00:01:18.730
because most of human communication
is actually non-verbal.

00:01:18.730 --> 00:01:21.840
Joe Navarro is actually an ex-FBI agent

00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:24.320
and he talks about all these unique tips

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:27.070
that you can use to read
people from across the room

00:01:27.070 --> 00:01:28.770
or even someone you're interacting with.

00:01:28.770 --> 00:01:30.380
Even the smallest body parts,

00:01:30.380 --> 00:01:33.449
eyelids, cheeks, fingers.

00:01:33.449 --> 00:01:35.630
- The name's Sherlock Holmes.

00:01:35.630 --> 00:01:37.319
- When I read this book
for the first time,

00:01:37.319 --> 00:01:38.790
it shocked me.

00:01:38.790 --> 00:01:40.570
I love people watching.

00:01:40.570 --> 00:01:44.150
I started understanding what
was going on in each situation

00:01:44.150 --> 00:01:46.510
without even needing to
hear what they were saying.

00:01:46.510 --> 00:01:50.070
The entire body gives us a
better picture than just the face

00:01:50.070 --> 00:01:52.100
and I'll use an example from the book

00:01:52.100 --> 00:01:54.660
where if you look at two
people having a conversation

00:01:54.660 --> 00:01:55.880
and you look at the direction

00:01:55.880 --> 00:01:57.720
of where their feet are pointing,

00:01:57.720 --> 00:01:59.880
that speaks volumes about how much

00:01:59.880 --> 00:02:01.650
they're enjoying that conversation.

00:02:01.650 --> 00:02:03.520
If you see two people
sitting next to each other

00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:06.500
and one person's feet are
pointing away from that person,

00:02:06.500 --> 00:02:08.710
they're ready to get out
of that conversation.

00:02:08.710 --> 00:02:11.930
Joe even goes as far as
to give you simple tips

00:02:11.930 --> 00:02:16.060
to change your posture to
allow you to express authority,

00:02:16.060 --> 00:02:17.660
to build trust with somebody else.

00:02:17.660 --> 00:02:20.170
If you put your hands
completely in your pockets,

00:02:20.170 --> 00:02:22.870
that's a very passive, submissive pose.

00:02:22.870 --> 00:02:25.620
Now, if you want to exemplify
a little bit of confidence,

00:02:25.620 --> 00:02:27.910
all you have to do is take your thumbs out

00:02:27.910 --> 00:02:30.240
and that just makes your body language

00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:31.547
speak with more authority.

00:02:31.547 --> 00:02:34.530
"Influence: the Psychology of Persuasion"

00:02:34.530 --> 00:02:36.770
by Robert Cialdini, Ph.D.

00:02:36.770 --> 00:02:38.840
I love reading books on influence

00:02:38.840 --> 00:02:41.220
and it's not because I want
to manipulate somebody.

00:02:41.220 --> 00:02:42.910
People often think that influence

00:02:42.910 --> 00:02:44.630
carries a negative connotation.

00:02:44.630 --> 00:02:45.832
I think it's more manipulation

00:02:45.832 --> 00:02:47.610
that carries the connotation.

00:02:47.610 --> 00:02:50.962
Manipulation by definition is a shrewd

00:02:50.962 --> 00:02:54.160
or devious way to influence
somebody for your own advantage.

00:02:54.160 --> 00:02:55.820
If a patient walks into my office,

00:02:55.820 --> 00:02:57.500
they have high cholesterol,
high blood pressure

00:02:57.500 --> 00:02:58.650
and they don't exercise,

00:02:58.650 --> 00:03:02.760
it's my job to influence them
to begin an exercise regimen.

00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:05.030
It doesn't mean that I'm manipulating them

00:03:05.030 --> 00:03:06.100
for my own advantage.

00:03:06.100 --> 00:03:09.360
Now in everyday life, we not
only need to be very good

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:11.080
at influencing people at our jobs,

00:03:11.080 --> 00:03:13.040
whether you're a doctor
influencing your patients,

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:15.000
or a teacher influencing your students,

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:17.580
we also need to be aware
of those who are trying

00:03:17.580 --> 00:03:20.636
to influence, or manipulate, us.

00:03:20.636 --> 00:03:22.862
- It's just a matter of luring him out.

00:03:22.862 --> 00:03:23.695
- Yehaw!

00:03:23.695 --> 00:03:26.410
- One of the concepts this
book breaks down very well

00:03:26.410 --> 00:03:28.950
is something known as
the contrast principle.

00:03:28.950 --> 00:03:32.100
And it's something that sales
people utilize all the time

00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:33.900
in order to get you to spend more money.

00:03:33.900 --> 00:03:35.490
I'm a sales person and you're coming

00:03:35.490 --> 00:03:37.010
into the store to buy a suit.

00:03:37.010 --> 00:03:40.390
I will get you committed
to buying this $400 suit

00:03:40.390 --> 00:03:43.250
and then I'll offer you an $80 sweater,

00:03:43.250 --> 00:03:45.100
which is a pretty expensive sweater,

00:03:45.100 --> 00:03:46.540
but it won't look as expensive

00:03:46.540 --> 00:03:48.840
because you've already spent $400.

00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:50.037
- Wow, that's messed up.

00:03:50.037 --> 00:03:52.940
- "The Influential Mind" by Tali Sharot.

00:03:52.940 --> 00:03:54.420
If you want someone to understand

00:03:54.420 --> 00:03:55.500
where you're coming from,

00:03:55.500 --> 00:03:58.350
it's gonna take a lot more
than facts and figures.

00:03:58.350 --> 00:03:59.183
Oh, I like that.

00:03:59.183 --> 00:04:00.016
That was a good.

00:04:00.016 --> 00:04:03.020
Most people are influenced by emotions

00:04:03.020 --> 00:04:05.080
rather than facts and figures.

00:04:05.080 --> 00:04:07.620
You can see this right
now on the news cycle.

00:04:07.620 --> 00:04:10.980
When somebody starts talking
to your primitive brain,

00:04:10.980 --> 00:04:13.280
they do a much better job at influencing

00:04:13.280 --> 00:04:15.250
your thoughts and even your actions.

00:04:15.250 --> 00:04:16.480
When your mind is stressed,

00:04:16.480 --> 00:04:18.690
it's much more easily influenced.

00:04:18.690 --> 00:04:21.340
And we see this after
horrible terrorist attacks

00:04:21.340 --> 00:04:24.810
like September 11th, it takes
one person to start running

00:04:24.810 --> 00:04:26.900
in order to get hundreds to do the same

00:04:26.900 --> 00:04:28.100
because in that stressed state,

00:04:28.100 --> 00:04:29.550
they're more likely to be influenced.

00:04:29.550 --> 00:04:31.490
I think the real gem in this book

00:04:31.490 --> 00:04:34.000
is that it teaches you how to communicate

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:36.030
with somebody that disagrees with you.

00:04:36.030 --> 00:04:39.130
The way you do that is you
establish a new concept

00:04:39.130 --> 00:04:41.720
that doesn't necessarily
disagree with their viewpoint

00:04:41.720 --> 00:04:43.310
but does agree with yours.

00:04:43.310 --> 00:04:45.500
Once you find that sort of common ground,

00:04:45.500 --> 00:04:47.520
it can facilitate that conversation

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:49.200
and allow you to exert your influence.

00:04:49.200 --> 00:04:50.720
If I'm trying to encourage a parent

00:04:50.720 --> 00:04:51.900
to vaccinate their child

00:04:51.900 --> 00:04:54.370
and they happen to be anti-vaccine,

00:04:54.370 --> 00:04:56.340
instead of going
back-and-forth and arguing

00:04:56.340 --> 00:04:58.280
about the side effects of vaccines,

00:04:58.280 --> 00:05:00.534
I introduce a new point
where we can both agree

00:05:00.534 --> 00:05:03.880
that the disease that the
vaccine prevents, like measles,

00:05:03.880 --> 00:05:05.280
is a bad disease.

00:05:05.280 --> 00:05:06.840
If the parent agrees with me,

00:05:06.840 --> 00:05:10.090
we have a much higher
likelihood of seeing eye-to-eye

00:05:10.090 --> 00:05:12.577
and agreeing on getting a
vaccination for their child.

00:05:12.577 --> 00:05:16.080
"Public Speaking for
Success" by Dale Carnegie.

00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:18.870
We all need to improve
how we speak in public.

00:05:18.870 --> 00:05:20.410
To be an effective communicator,

00:05:20.410 --> 00:05:22.990
to share your story, to influence people,

00:05:22.990 --> 00:05:24.850
to be a better person yourself,

00:05:24.850 --> 00:05:27.010
you need to be able to be
a better public speaker.

00:05:27.010 --> 00:05:30.650
Dale Carnegie gives you some
really simple steps that anyone

00:05:30.650 --> 00:05:33.400
can follow in order to be
a better public speaker.

00:05:33.400 --> 00:05:35.410
One of those steps that I take to heart

00:05:35.410 --> 00:05:37.300
and use in all of my speeches

00:05:37.300 --> 00:05:40.340
is the idea of verbalizing
your speech or your talk

00:05:40.340 --> 00:05:42.010
before even doing it.

00:05:42.010 --> 00:05:44.270
That way, your brain can
recite the information,

00:05:44.270 --> 00:05:45.810
get used to saying it.

00:05:45.810 --> 00:05:47.980
You'll probably stumble and that's okay

00:05:47.980 --> 00:05:50.860
because you're practicing
but you have to practice.

00:05:50.860 --> 00:05:52.643
It talks about really great speakers,

00:05:52.643 --> 00:05:56.270
how they presented their
speeches, what words they used,

00:05:56.270 --> 00:05:59.560
why certain speeches were
more effective than others

00:05:59.560 --> 00:06:01.390
and that really goes a long way

00:06:01.390 --> 00:06:03.407
in order for you to
communicate your ideas.

00:06:03.407 --> 00:06:07.050
"Flow: The Psychology
of Optimal Experience"

00:06:07.050 --> 00:06:08.500
by Mihaly (mumbles) (laughs).

00:06:11.850 --> 00:06:12.777
Even though this book is called

00:06:12.777 --> 00:06:14.860
"The Psychology of Optimal Experience,"

00:06:14.860 --> 00:06:17.200
it's basically a book about happiness.

00:06:17.200 --> 00:06:19.280
This is an evolving field of psychology

00:06:19.280 --> 00:06:21.230
known as positive psychology.

00:06:21.230 --> 00:06:22.960
And this is one of the first books

00:06:22.960 --> 00:06:24.290
I've read on the subject

00:06:24.290 --> 00:06:26.230
and I've become obsessed.

00:06:26.230 --> 00:06:28.850
In medicine, we always look
at the negative symptoms,

00:06:28.850 --> 00:06:31.170
we talk about depression, anxiety,

00:06:31.170 --> 00:06:33.880
bipolar disorder, but positive psychology

00:06:33.880 --> 00:06:36.480
focuses on the positive aspects of life.

00:06:36.480 --> 00:06:37.670
What is flow?

00:06:37.670 --> 00:06:38.890
When you are playing basketball,

00:06:38.890 --> 00:06:39.920
when you are rock climbing,

00:06:39.920 --> 00:06:41.210
when you are playing an instrument,

00:06:41.210 --> 00:06:43.320
you're putting in a lot of effort

00:06:43.320 --> 00:06:46.110
and generally, for human
survival sake at least,

00:06:46.110 --> 00:06:47.350
when we put in effort

00:06:47.350 --> 00:06:51.260
it's done so in order to
get something in return

00:06:51.260 --> 00:06:54.340
whether that's financial
return, sexual return,

00:06:54.340 --> 00:06:56.330
that feeling you feel when
you're climbing a mountain,

00:06:56.330 --> 00:06:57.860
playing an instrument, playing basketball,

00:06:57.860 --> 00:06:59.320
is called flow.

00:06:59.320 --> 00:07:02.090
It's a very self-fulfilling, authentic

00:07:02.090 --> 00:07:03.440
happiness that you feel.

00:07:03.440 --> 00:07:05.500
When people look at my
Instagram or YouTube channels,

00:07:05.500 --> 00:07:07.480
they always comment on how happy I look

00:07:07.480 --> 00:07:09.460
and they say that they don't believe

00:07:09.460 --> 00:07:10.950
that I'm always so happy

00:07:10.950 --> 00:07:14.300
or that they want to know
how I'm always so happy.

00:07:14.300 --> 00:07:17.820
I think flow is a great
reason of why I'm happy.

00:07:17.820 --> 00:07:20.300
Flow explains that in
order to feel happiness,

00:07:20.300 --> 00:07:22.550
to get that intrinsic joy in life,

00:07:22.550 --> 00:07:25.450
you constantly need to set
these little achievable

00:07:25.450 --> 00:07:27.600
and meaningful goals for yourself

00:07:27.600 --> 00:07:29.320
whether we're talking
about cleaning the house

00:07:29.320 --> 00:07:30.750
in a certain amount of time,

00:07:30.750 --> 00:07:33.730
or reading a certain amount
of books in a given year.

00:07:33.730 --> 00:07:36.650
A great paradox that this book addresses

00:07:36.650 --> 00:07:39.630
is that we don't spend our free time well.

00:07:39.630 --> 00:07:42.010
Most people can't wait to leave work.

00:07:42.010 --> 00:07:44.940
They can't wait to
retire but when they do,

00:07:44.940 --> 00:07:48.000
they actually become more
unhappy and more anxiety sets in.

00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:50.560
When you are not activating your mind

00:07:50.560 --> 00:07:53.020
to reach goals and set
challenges for yourself,

00:07:53.020 --> 00:07:55.630
like you are every time you're at work,

00:07:55.630 --> 00:07:58.810
your mind fills that
empty space with anxiety.

00:07:58.810 --> 00:07:59.960
When you have anxiety,

00:07:59.960 --> 00:08:02.280
that leads to more
problems, more depression.

00:08:02.280 --> 00:08:04.140
When you're at work and
you're constantly setting

00:08:04.140 --> 00:08:06.560
new goals and new obstacles for yourself,

00:08:06.560 --> 00:08:08.240
you're able to reach those goals

00:08:08.240 --> 00:08:10.770
and therefore feel that
reward system kicking in.

